<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  App target. Belongs at the root of the App bundle, which is what adding it to the
  target's Copy Bundle Resources phase does. docs/IOS.md step 4 wires it up.

  Read Apple's reason texts before touching the codes below: several codes share a
  category and they are not interchangeable, and the obvious-looking one is often
  about *displaying* the value rather than using it.

  FileTimestamp
    C617.1 — metadata of files inside the app container, the app group container or
      the CloudKit container. That is MeteoRideShareStore pruning the shared inbox: it
      reads `.modificationDate` to drop anything the web layer never collected in 24
      hours, and the folder is in the App Group container. It is also the reason
      @capacitor/filesystem's README asks for, and for the same kind of access.
      Not DDA9.1, which is for *displaying* a timestamp to the person — nothing here
      shows one. Not 3B52.1 either, which is for files the person specifically granted
      access to through something like a document picker; the inbox is the app's own.

  UserDefaults
    CA92.1 — user defaults readable only by this app. @capacitor/preferences writes
      `UserDefaults.standard` and its `group` option is a key prefix, not a suite name
      (node_modules/@capacitor/preferences/ios/Sources/PreferencesPlugin/Preferences.swift),
      so nothing here shares defaults through the App Group. Not 1C8F.1, which is for
      defaults shared with an App Group, and emphatically not 54BD.1, which is about
      active keyboard information and has nothing to do with user defaults.

  The collected-data section must agree with the App Privacy answers in App Store
  Connect; public/privacy-ios.html is what both are filled from. Precise location covers
  the route's own coordinates, which go to Open-Meteo on every forecast — the device's
  position is read only to centre the map and never leaves. Other data covers the
  OpenWeather key, which the user supplies and which identifies them to OpenWeather.
  "Data not collected" would be false: Open-Meteo's logs may hold coordinates for 90
  days, so this is not ephemeral processing either.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
